<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">United Kingdom of the Netherlands</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1839 CE · capital Amsterdam</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom created at the Congress of Vienna to unite the northern and southern Low Countries under William I, broken apart by the Belgian Revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> joined the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austrian-netherlands">Austrian Netherlands</a> into one kingdom as a barrier against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. William I, sovereign prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sovereign-principality-of-the-united-netherlands">Sovereign Principality of the United Netherlands</a>, proclaimed himself king in March 1815, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and allied troops fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> that June. He also became grand duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>William I ruled energetically, with the capital alternating between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>. He founded universities at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/societe-generale-de-belgique">Société Générale</a> bank and supported industry such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cockerill">Cockerill</a>'s ironworks. But the south, more populous and largely Catholic and French-speaking among its elites, resented Dutch-dominated government, language policy and taxes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a riot at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> opera performance in August 1830, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> drove out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> troops and created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium</a>. William refused to accept this until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> in 1839, which left the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a> in the north.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-kingdom-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="United Kingdom of the Netherlands" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1839 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amsterdam</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William I of the Netherlands</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the United Netherlands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1827&amp;focus=united-kingdom-of-the-netherlands" aria-label="United Kingdom of the Netherlands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4790 370 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United Kingdom of the Netherlands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5110 626L5110 626M5110 626L5097 634M5110 626L5110 626M5110 626L5099 670M5110 626L5141 706M5110 626L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5110 626h0M5097 634h0M5110 626h0M5099 670h0M5141 706h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5110 626h0"